# 502 - Universal Class Trainers

## Status
- Created: 2026-04-02
- Reopened: 2026-09-03 (dropped note, folded in below)
- Decided 2026-09-03: per-class generated spell siblings answer the resource
  wall; the seven currency names are mapped to the seven engine power slots;
  costs convert as a percentage of the engagement rather than an exchange
  rate; rage is priced as an accrual currency alongside runic power; scope is
  class trainers only
- Blocked-ish 2026-09-03: the traveller system that would deliver these
  trainers is not loaded on the active profile — see *The delivery problem*
- Phase: 5 (Friendly Encounters — the trainers who wander by)
- Priority: Medium

## Origin

The original 2026-04-02 filing asked for one thing: let a warrior open a mage
trainer's window. A note dropped into the project root on 2026-09-03 asks for
the same thing plus two more, and is preserved here word for word because it
sets the shape of the whole design:

> hello! I think it'd be neat if there was a config file option in the worldserver
> that let all trainer-taught skills be trainable to any class. Then, limit them
> by gold, and let everyone train everything eventually.

Three requests, in order of how deep they cut:

1. **A worldserver config option** — not a hardcoded patch, not a Lua hook.
   A key in `worldserver.conf` that an operator flips, so the vanilla profile
   can run open trainers while release runs retail rules from the same binary.
2. **All trainer-taught skills, any class** — the class gate comes off.
3. **Gold as the only limit, and "eventually" everything** — the wall is not
   "you are the wrong class", it is "you cannot afford it yet". Money becomes
   the pacing mechanism, and a rich enough character owns every skill in the
   game.

## Current Behavior

A trainer refuses the wrong class twice, at two different depths, and both
refusals have to be lifted for the note's request to work.

### Gate one — the whole trainer says no

`Trainer::IsTrainerValidForPlayer` in
`source-beta/src/server/game/Entities/Creature/Trainer.cpp` compares the
player against the `Requirement` column of the `trainer` table, dispatching on
the `Type` column:

| `trainer.Type` | Name       | What `Requirement` is compared against |
| -------------- | ---------- | -------------------------------------- |
| 0              | Class      | the player's class                     |
| 1              | Mount      | the player's race                      |
| 2              | Tradeskill | a spell the player must already know   |
| 3              | Pet        | the player's class                     |

A `Requirement` of 0 means no gate at all. This function is consulted from two
places — `WorldSession::SendTrainerList` in
`source-beta/src/server/game/Handlers/NPCHandler.cpp`, which is what builds the
training window, and the trainer gossip-option builder in
`source-beta/src/server/game/Entities/Player/PlayerGossip.cpp`, which is what
decides whether the "I seek training" line appears in the NPC's dialogue at
all. Fail this gate and the player never sees a menu entry.

### Gate two — each individual spell says no

Even inside a trainer window the player is allowed to open,
`Trainer::SendSpells` skips any spell where
`Player::IsSpellFitByClassAndRace` returns false, and
`Trainer::GetSpellState` returns `Unavailable` for the same reason. That
function (`source-beta/src/server/game/Entities/Player/Player.cpp`) walks the
skill-line-ability entries for the spell and checks three masks: the spell's
race mask against the player's race, the spell's class mask against the
player's class, and `SkillRaceClassInfo.dbc` for whether that skill line is
even offered to this race/class pair. A spell with no skill-line entry at all
passes automatically — which is why first aid and cooking are already
classless.

So gate one is per-NPC and gate two is per-spell. Removing only gate one gets
a warrior a mage trainer window containing nothing.

### What already limits by gold

`Trainer::TeachSpell` already charges. It reads `MoneyCost` from the
`trainer_spell` row, multiplies it by `Player::GetReputationPriceDiscount`,
refuses with `FailReason::NotEnoughMoney` if the purse is short, and calls
`ModifyMoney` with the negative. The gold pipe the note wants to lean on
exists; nothing new has to be built to charge for a spell, only to charge
*differently* for an out-of-class one.

### What the config system looks like today

This AzerothCore build caches config values rather than re-reading the file.
`enum ServerConfigs` in
`source-beta/src/server/game/World/WorldConfig.h` is one flat enum of every
setting, terminated by `MAX_NUM_SERVER_CONFIGS`.
`WorldConfig::BuildConfigCache` in the matching `.cpp` binds each enum entry to
its `worldserver.conf` key name and default with a `SetConfigValue<T>` call,
optionally marking it `Reloadable::Yes` so `.reload config` picks it up
without a restart. Call sites read it back through `sWorld->getBoolConfig`,
`getIntConfig`, or `getFloatConfig`. `CONFIG_ALL_TAXI_PATHS` / `AllFlightPaths`
is the closest existing model: one bool, read once at a decision point.

## Intended Behavior

### The switch

Four new keys in `worldserver.conf`, all defaulting to today's retail
behavior so that a profile which does not opt in is unchanged:

| Key                              | Type  | Default | Effect                                                        |
| -------------------------------- | ----- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Trainer.AnyClass`               | bool  | 0       | master switch; off means every rule below is inert            |
| `Trainer.AnyClass.TypeMask`      | int   | 1       | which `trainer.Type` values lose their requirement check      |
| `Trainer.AnyClass.CostMultiplier`| float | 1.0     | gold multiplier applied to a spell outside the player's class |
| `Trainer.AnyClass.CostPerKnown`  | float | 0.0     | extra multiplier per out-of-class spell already owned         |

`TypeMask` is a bitmask over the trainer types above — bit 0 Class, bit 1
Mount, bit 2 Tradeskill, bit 3 Pet — so an operator can open class training
without also handing every race every mount. Default 1 opens class trainers
only, and that is the decided scope (2026-09-03): mount trainers keep their
race requirement, pet trainers keep their class requirement, profession
trainers keep their prerequisite spell. The mask exists so a later operator
can widen it without another source patch, not because this issue intends to.

### What a player sees

A level 12 warrior meets a wandering mage trainer. The gossip menu offers
training. The window lists the mage's full spell list, level-gated as normal,
but Frostbolt costs (say) four times what it costs a mage, and the fifth
mage spell this warrior buys costs more than the first did. Nothing is
greyed out for being the wrong class. Given enough copper, over enough
wandering trainers, the warrior ends up holding every spell in the game —
which is the "eventually" the note asks for.

### The two costs, and why there are two

`CostMultiplier` is a flat surcharge: an out-of-class spell is simply
expensive. `CostPerKnown` is the escalator: each out-of-class spell already
learned raises the price of the next one, so the first dabble is cheap and
the hundredth is ruinous. With `CostPerKnown` at 0 the escalator is off and
the surcharge is flat, which is the simplest thing that satisfies the note.
The escalator exists because "everyone trains everything eventually" and
"a level 12 warrior should not be a mage by Tuesday" are both true, and a
curve is the only way to hold both.

Both multipliers apply *after* the reputation discount and only to spells
that `IsSpellFitByClassAndRace` would have rejected — in-class training keeps
its exact current price, so a mage's own spells cost a mage what they always
did.

### The resource wall — the thing that makes this not work by itself

A warrior who buys Frostbolt cannot cast it, and the reason is not a check
that can be switched off.

A character's power type comes from `ChrClasses.dbc`; `Player::InitStatsForLevel`
and the shapeshift path in `Player.cpp` both read `cEntry->powerType` and call
`setPowerType` with it. A warrior is `POWER_RAGE` and has a maximum mana of
zero. `Spell::CheckPower` in
`source-beta/src/server/game/Spells/Spell.cpp` ends in a plain comparison —
`GetPower(PowerType) < m_powerCost` — and returns `SPELL_FAILED_NO_POWER`.
Zero is less than any cost. The spell sits in the spellbook, fully learned,
and fails on every cast.

This cuts both ways. A mage who buys Mortal Strike has no rage; a rogue who
buys anything of anyone's has no mana; and a warrior who buys Sinister Strike
has no energy. Roughly, the skills that work cross-class out of the box are
the ones that cost nothing or cost health: weapon skills, riding, professions,
first aid, and passive or utility spells. The skills that define a class —
its damage abilities — are exactly the ones the resource wall stops.

There is one upstream lever already in the tree:
`ScriptMgr::OnPlayerHasActivePowerType`, hook
`PLAYERHOOK_ON_PLAYER_HAS_ACTIVE_POWER_TYPE`, lets a module answer "yes this
player has that power type" for the drain, restore and regeneration paths.
It does *not* raise the maximum, so it does not by itself let the spell cast.
It is noted here only so a later reader does not spend an afternoon on it.

### The answer — a spell costs every currency at once

Decided 2026-09-03, in the user's own words, kept exactly as given:

> Rewrite the spell's power costs to cost either N mana, M energy, R rage,
> T tiempo, J james, I important, and R runes etc.

The move is to stop treating a spell's cost as a *pair* — one power type,
one amount — and start treating it as a price quoted in seven denominations.
Frostbolt does not cost mana. It costs N mana **and** M energy **and** R rage
**and** T tiempo, all at once, and each caster settles the term it is able to
settle.

Under that reading the resource wall dissolves without anyone being handed a
mana bar they should not have. A mage casting Frostbolt settles the mana term
because mana is what a mage keeps. A warrior casting the same Frostbolt
settles the rage term, out of the rage a warrior keeps, and the mana term is
simply not the term that binds them. The spell is one price written in seven
denominations, and the class you are decides which denomination you happen to
be holding.

This also reframes what "expensive" means. Gold, per the note, is the wall
between you and *owning* a skill. The seven-denomination price is the wall
between you and *using* it — a warrior's Frostbolt is not free just because
the training bill was paid; it is priced in rage every single cast, and rage
is the currency a warrior needs for everything else.

The denominations are settled below in *The seven slots*. **How** a caster
comes to be holding the right denomination is settled in *How the cost gets
rewritten* — not by re-pricing the spell at cast time, but by handing each
class its own copy of the spell with the price already converted.

#### The seven slots, and the mapping

The engine's `enum Powers` in `source-beta/src/server/shared/SharedDefines.h`
has exactly seven real entries before `MAX_POWERS`. The list given on
2026-09-03 is also exactly seven long. The names were given as approximate —
"I had the wrong names but we picked the right number so just map them" — so
the four that already correspond to engine slots take those slots, and the
three that do not are assigned, in the order they were given, to the three
slots the list otherwise leaves unnamed:

| Index | Engine name         | The given list | Where the name came from        |
| ----- | ------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------- |
| 0     | `POWER_MANA`        | N mana         | matches outright                |
| 1     | `POWER_RAGE`        | R rage         | matches outright                |
| 2     | `POWER_FOCUS`       | T tiempo       | assigned, order-preserving      |
| 3     | `POWER_ENERGY`      | M energy       | matches outright                |
| 4     | `POWER_HAPPINESS`   | J james        | assigned, order-preserving      |
| 5     | `POWER_RUNE`        | R runes        | matches outright                |
| 6     | `POWER_RUNIC_POWER` | I important    | assigned, order-preserving      |

The assignment preserves the sequence as given — tiempo, james, important sat
in that order between rage and runes, and focus, happiness, runic power are
the three free slots in that same order. Each also lands somewhere it reads
truthfully: *tiempo* on focus, which in 3.3.5 is the one pool that refills on
nothing but elapsed time; *james* on happiness, which is a mood and the only
slot in the table that belongs to a creature rather than a number; and
*important* on runic power, which is what a death knight spends on the moves
that finish a fight rather than the ones that start it.

Two of these seven belong to nobody a player can be. **Focus and happiness are
hunter-pet resources** — no player character of any class holds either. That
leaves four slots that a player can actually pay from: mana (eight classes),
rage (warriors, and druids in bear form), energy (rogues, and druids in cat
form), and runic power (death knights). The naming is now complete across all
seven; the generation below only ever needs to reach for those four.

One observation left standing, not acted on: in five of the seven entries the
coefficient letter is the currency's own first letter — **T** tiempo, **J**
james, **I** important, **R** rage, **R** runes. Mana and energy swap onto
each other's initials (**N** mana, **M** energy), and R serves both rage and
runes. If those letters are a notation rather than placeholders, the notation
should be written down before anyone builds the table.

### How the cost gets rewritten — per-class copies of the spell

Decided 2026-09-03, in the user's own words:

> yes we can modify the spell when the player learns it to cost an equivalent
> amount of mana/energy/rage/etc. So a new version can be programmatically
> generated, one for each class, and we unlearn the default one and learn the
> class-specific one to the character-client.

So the spell is not re-priced at cast time. It is re-priced **once, at learn
time, by handing the player a different spell.** Frostbolt keeps costing mana
forever, for everybody, because Frostbolt is the mage's. What a warrior walks
away from the trainer holding is a *generated sibling* of Frostbolt — same
effects, same school, same family flags, same everything, with two fields
changed: the power type is rage instead of mana, and the amount is whatever
rage is worth against that much mana. The trainer teaches the stock spell; the
server immediately unlearns it and learns the warrior's copy in its place.

This is a better shape than re-pricing at cast time, and for a reason that is
not obvious: **the client enforces cost too.** The 3.3.5 client greys out an
action button when it believes the caster cannot pay, using the cost written
in its own `Spell.dbc`. A warrior with a maximum mana of zero holding a spell
the client thinks costs mana sees a permanently dark button and never sends
the cast at all — so no amount of server-side leniency in `Spell::CheckPower`
would have made it castable. Only a spell whose *data* says rage gets the
client to agree. The generated-sibling route is the one that works.

#### The generation mechanism already exists

`LoadDBC` in `source-beta/src/server/game/DataStores/DBCStores.cpp` loads
`Spell.dbc` from disk and then calls `storage.LoadFromDB("spell_dbc", ...)`,
merging rows from the world database into the in-memory spell store. Upstream
already uses this table to rewrite spell rows — see the `UPDATE spell_dbc`
statements in `source-beta/data/sql/archive/db_world/`. New spell entries are
therefore pure SQL on the server side; no engine patch is needed to bring them
into existence.

The fields a sibling changes are `SpellEntry::PowerType` (DBC field 41), which
becomes the target class's own power, and the cost fields alongside it —
`ManaCost` (42), `ManaCostPerlevel` (43), `ManaPerSecond` (44) and
`ManaCostPercentage` (204), all in
`source-beta/src/server/shared/DataStores/DBCStructure.h`. Everything else in
the row is copied verbatim.

#### "An equivalent amount" — the cost is a percentage, not a number

Decided 2026-09-03, in the user's own words:

> it's not a flat rate, but the benefit of energy for our cases is that it's
> roughly E% of the resources that unit will be using in the engagement. The
> starting part of it. So, it's probably about as much as one mage spell of
> similar function. They have cast times and such...

The conversion is a **fraction of the engagement**, not an exchange rate
between pools. A spell does not cost 300 of something; it costs *this much of
what you were going to spend in this fight*. Energy is the yardstick because
energy is already denominated that way — the pool caps at 100, so a 40-energy
ability costs, on its face, 40% of the bar, and nobody has to convert anything
to see it. Rage reads the same way. Mana does not, because mana runs to
thousands, but mana is the one that has to be translated, not the standard.

The calibration target is stated in the same breath: one converted cast should
cost about what **one mage spell of similar function** costs its own class.
Not a derived number — a comparison. The generator's conversion table is
correct when a warrior's borrowed Frostbolt takes about the same bite out of a
warrior's fight that Frostbolt takes out of a mage's.

##### Cast time is already paid, and does not need converting

"They have cast times and such" is the other half of the price, and it turns
out the design gets it for free. The sibling is a wholesale copy, so
`CastingTimeIndex` comes across unchanged — a warrior's Frostbolt still takes
the same seconds to cast that a mage's does, seconds in which nothing else
happens. The time component of a spell's cost is therefore *already identical*
across every class holding it, with no conversion needed and no way to get it
wrong. Only the resource component has to be translated. That is a smaller
problem than it first looked.

It also means the two components stay honestly coupled. A borrowed spell does
not become a rogue-paced instant just because a rogue learned it; it stays as
slow as it was written, and the rogue pays energy *and* stands still for the
cast. The spell keeps its shape in everyone's hands.

##### The engine already speaks percentages

`SpellEntry::ManaCostPercentage` is DBC field 204, and
`SpellInfo::CalcPowerCost` in
`source-beta/src/server/game/Spells/SpellInfo.cpp` already resolves it
per-caster at cast time:

| Power type                       | What the percentage is taken of         |
| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `POWER_MANA`                     | `GetCreateMana()` — base mana for class and level |
| `POWER_RAGE` `POWER_ENERGY` `POWER_FOCUS` `POWER_HAPPINESS` | `GetMaxPower()` — the pool's cap |
| `POWER_RUNE` `POWER_RUNIC_POWER` | nothing — see below                     |

So the generator does not have to compute per-class numbers at all. It emits
`ManaCost = 0` and `ManaCostPercentage = E`, and the engine converts for
whoever is casting, at the moment of casting, forever. The percentage
self-adjusts as the character levels, which absolute numbers would not.

Two properties of that worth knowing, because both are load-bearing:

- **The client reads field 204 too.** Its greyed-button logic uses the same
  percentage from its own `Spell.dbc`, so client and server agree without
  anything being kept in sync by hand. This is the second reason the sibling
  design beats a runtime cost loop: a loop would have left the client
  computing a different price than the server charged.
- **Mana percentages are of *base* mana, not max mana.** `GetCreateMana()`
  excludes the intellect on your gear. A percentage-priced spell therefore
  stays the same fraction of your intrinsic pool no matter how you gear, and
  does not quietly get cheaper as a mage stacks intellect. That is almost
  certainly what is wanted, and it is not obvious from the outside, so it is
  written down here.

##### Death knights are free unless something is patched

The switch in `CalcPowerCost` reaches `POWER_RUNE` and `POWER_RUNIC_POWER` and
logs `"CalculateManaCost: Not implemented yet!"`, adding nothing to the cost.
A percentage-priced spell therefore costs a death knight **zero**. Every
cross-class spell, free, forever.

Two ways out, and one of them is four lines: extend that switch to take a
percentage of `GetMaxPower(POWER_RUNIC_POWER)` — which is 1000 internally, set
in `Player::InitStatsForLevel` alongside the eight runes — the same way rage
and energy are already handled. The other is to give death-knight siblings
absolute costs while everyone else gets percentages, which means two code
paths in the generator and a class that drifts out of balance separately. The
four-line patch is the recommendation, and it belongs in the same
`patches/B*.sh` as the rest.

##### Runes and runic power — which spells take which

Death knights are the only class holding two currencies at once, so their
siblings need a rule the other nine do not. The rule, as given on 2026-09-03:
powerful spells that demand a lot of resource take one of the **top two**
runes; spells on a short cooldown of four or six seconds take one currency;
and spells with no cooldown or a long one take the other.

**"Top two" needs an ordering, and the ordering was to come from the specs'
own icons.** The three spec icons are named by the game's own data rather than
guessed at: `TalentTab.dbc` gives each death-knight tab a `SpellIconID`, and
`SpellIcon.dbc` resolves those to texture paths.

| Talent tab | Order | Icon id | Texture |
| ---------- | ----- | ------- | ------- |
| Blood      | 0     | 2636    | `Interface\Icons\Spell_Deathknight_BloodPresence` |
| Frost      | 1     | 2632    | `Interface\Icons\Spell_Deathknight_FrostPresence` |
| Unholy     | 2     | 2633    | `Interface\Icons\Spell_Deathknight_UnholyPresence` |

All three were pulled out of the client's `Data/enUS/locale-enUS.MPQ` — the
base archives carry the sounds and the spell models, but interface art lives
in the locale archive — and averaged as 64x64 RGBA. The frame is identical on
all three icons and drags every average down by the same amount, so the second
column drops the five-pixel engraved border and averages only the art:

| Spec   | Whole icon        | Art only          | Luminance | Saturation |
| ------ | ----------------- | ----------------- | --------- | ---------- |
| Blood  | `#68342E`         | `#7D372F`         | 69.5      | 62.6%      |
| Frost  | `#4B5172`         | `#575F89`         | 96.2      | 36.7%      |
| Unholy | `#466339`         | `#4F763D`         | 105.6     | 48.3%      |

**What the colours are for was misread the first time, and the correction is
the whole idea.** The colours do not rank the specs against each other. They
are anchors, and each spell is assigned to whichever anchor **its own icon's
colour is nearest to**. As given on 2026-09-03:

> the icons of the spells that we're assigning, look at the colors, and the
> colors that are close to them should be part of that spec. So like... an
> unholy death knight would cast nature spells that are green. And a holy
> paladin would cast fire spells that are white and yellow and red.

Which is a rule a machine can apply to all 49,839 rows of `Spell.dbc` without
anyone hand-sorting a spell list. Each spell carries a `SpellIconID` in field
133; `SpellIcon.dbc` resolves it to a texture; the texture averages to a
colour; the nearest anchor wins.

##### It was tested on twenty spells and it works

Measured, not asserted. Icon averages against the three anchors, by CIE76
distance in Lab and separately by hue angle:

| Spell | Icon avg | Hue | By Lab | By hue |
| ----- | -------- | --- | ------ | ------ |
| Starfire | `#4C5587` | 231° | Frost 6.0 | Frost 0.2 |
| Corruption | `#814B4A` | 1° | Blood 13.0 | Blood 5.5 |
| Eviscerate | `#74483A` | 14° | Blood 13.3 | Blood 7.6 |
| Frostbolt | `#37355B` | 243° | Frost 17.3 | Frost 12.2 |
| Lightning Bolt | `#152A5F` | 223° | Frost 24.8 | Frost 7.5 |
| Fireball | `#9F673C` | 26° | Blood 24.1 | Blood 19.5 |
| Immolate | `#925A21` | 30° | Blood 26.6 | Blood 23.9 |
| Healing Touch | `#529175` | 153° | Unholy 20.9 | Unholy 52.3 |
| Wrath | `#42C09F` | 164° | Unholy 36.8 | Unholy 63.9 |
| Holy Light | `#CCAA61` | 41° | Unholy 41.2 | **Blood 34.3** |

The brief's own two examples both come out right. **Wrath and Healing Touch —
nature spells, green icons — land on unholy**, which is precisely "an unholy
death knight would cast nature spells that are green". **Fireball and Immolate
land on blood.** And Holy Light, gold at 41°, lands on blood under hue
distance — "white and yellow and red" — which is the paladin half of the
example, arriving on its own from the measurement.

##### Hue is the better metric, with saturation as a confidence gate

The two metrics agree on eighteen of twenty. Where they differ, hue is right
both times, and the reason is structural: Lab distance includes lightness, and
every icon shares the same dark engraved frame, so lightness is mostly noise
about the frame rather than signal about the spell. The rule as given is about
*colour* — green, white, yellow, red — and hue is the axis that word names.

But hue alone breaks on icons that are nearly grey, where the hue angle is
arbitrary:

| Spell | Icon avg | Saturation | Verdict |
| ----- | -------- | ---------- | ------- |
| Wrath | `#42C09F` | 66% | strong colour identity |
| Holy Light | `#CCAA61` | 52% | strong |
| Eviscerate | `#74483A` | 50% | strong |
| Frostbolt | `#37355B` | 41% | strong |
| Mind Blast | `#A27B78` | 26% | near-grey, hue is noise |
| Shadow Bolt | `#453547` | 25% | near-grey, hue is noise |
| Mortal Strike | `#5B5B70` | 19% | near-grey, hue is noise |

A threshold around 30% cleanly separates them, and the spells it rejects are
the ones whose assignments looked wrong: Shadow Bolt landing on frost, Mortal
Strike landing on frost. Those are not failures of the rule — they are spells
with **no colour identity at all**, and a physical warrior strike arguably
should not have an elemental spec. So the rule is: assign by hue when the
icon's saturation clears the gate, and when it does not, the spell has no
spec and falls back to whatever the default is.

##### Why this generalises past death knights

Nothing in the rule is specific to runes. Any class whose specs have icons —
which is all of them, via `TalentTab.dbc` — gets the same treatment: measure
the three spec icons, measure the spell's icon, assign by nearest hue. That
turns "which of this class's flavours does a borrowed spell belong to" from a
design question requiring ten hand-built tables into one function over data the
game already ships. It is the most reusable thing in this issue.

**The cooldown split as written names one currency twice.****The cooldown split as written names one currency twice.** "Short cooldowns
like 4 or 6 seconds cost runic power, and the other spells (no cooldown or
long cooldown) cost runic power" leaves nothing for the runes to pay for. One
of those two branches was meant to be runes. It is recorded as given rather
than silently corrected, and it is Open Question 4.

For what it is worth, the runes are themselves a cooldown-shaped currency —
each recharges on a timer rather than being spent from a pool — which argues
that the short-cooldown branch is the rune branch, and that a spell with no
cooldown is the one which should have to be paid for out of accumulated runic
power. That is a reading, not a decision.

##### What rage does at the start of a fight, which is nothing

The user's phrase was "the starting part of it", and rage is where that phrase
bites. A rogue opens an engagement with a full energy bar. A mage opens with
full mana. **A warrior opens with zero rage** — the pool starts empty and
fills only by hitting and being hit.

So a percentage-of-pool rage cost is not payable at the opening of a fight at
all. It becomes payable a few seconds in. This is not a bug to design around;
it is a rhythm the conversion imposes for free, and it is a good one: a mage
who bought a rogue ability can open with it instantly, and a warrior who
bought Frostbolt has to land a swing before the borrowed magic is available.
Every class's relationship with its own resource carries over into the
spells it borrows.

The consequence for the conversion table is a definitional one. "The resources
that unit will be using in the engagement" has to be measured **across** the
engagement for rage rather than at its start, because at its start there are
none. Energy and mana can be read off the bar; rage has to be read off the
accrual.

##### Rage is priced as runic power, not as energy

Decided 2026-09-03: *"warriors should use the same style for rage power costs
as runic power for deathknights. Same paradigm."*

This settles the asymmetry above by naming which side rage belongs on. The
four payable pools split two and two, not four ways:

| | **Stock currencies** | **Accrual currencies** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Pools** | mana, energy | rage, runic power |
| **At the opening of a fight** | full | empty |
| **Refilled by** | time, and by drinking | fighting |
| **Budget measured as** | what is in the bar | what accrues across the engagement |
| **A spell priced here** | is castable immediately | has to be earned first |

Rage and runic power are the same kind of thing wearing different names, so
they get one formula between them rather than two. A warrior's borrowed
Frostbolt and a death knight's borrowed Frostbolt are priced by the same rule,
against the same definition of a budget, and both have to be paid for out of
the fight rather than out of the bar. Whatever calibration is chosen for one
applies unchanged to the other, which halves the tuning surface and removes
the possibility of the two drifting apart.

It also means the death-knight rune rules above are the *general* accrual
rules, not a death-knight special case. Runic power is one accrual currency
and rage is the other; anything the cooldown split decides about runic power
is decided about rage in the same breath.

#### What the swap has to carry with it

Learning a different spell id than the one the game thinks you learned breaks
every check written against the original id, and there are three that matter:

- **Rank chains.** `Trainer::GetSpellState` refuses a spell when
  `sSpellMgr->GetPrevSpellInChain` names a previous rank the player does not
  have. A warrior holding warrior-Frostbolt-rank-1 does not have
  Frostbolt-rank-1, so rank 2 is refused forever. The generated siblings need
  their own chains in `spell_ranks`, one per class, parallel to the original.
- **Anything that asks `HasSpell` by number.** Quest rewards, `spell_required`,
  the ability-tome system in issue 405, the pretrain tables from 148j — all of
  them name original ids.
- **Talents and procs.** These key off `SpellFamilyName` and
  `SpellFamilyFlags` rather than the id, so a sibling copied wholesale keeps
  working. This is the one place the wholesale copy pays off, and it is a
  reason to copy every field rather than construct a new row.

#### The client still has to be told

A spell id the client's own `Spell.dbc` has never heard of has no name, no
icon, no tooltip and no cast. It cannot appear in the spellbook, because the
spellbook is drawn from client data — the server sends ids, not spells. So
every generated sibling has to reach the player's install, which means a
client-side patch archive, the same requirement issue 715 hit for its
per-level spell variants.

Issue 715 states the consequence more softly than this — "tooltips will be
wrong without client patch" — which reads as though the spell would still
appear, merely mislabelled. Those two readings differ and only one can be
true. It has not been checked in-game here, and it should be, because if 715
is right the client patch is a polish item and if this file is right it is a
blocking dependency. See Open Question 3.

#### How many spells this makes

The product is (spells a player may learn cross-class) x (classes). Issue 715
proposes multiplying the same set by *level* as well, and if both ship the
generated set is level x class, which is a different order of magnitude
entirely. Rather than guess at either number here, count them:

```sql
-- distinct spells offered by class trainers, from the live world DB
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT ts.SpellId)
FROM   trainer_spell ts
JOIN   trainer t ON t.Id = ts.TrainerId
WHERE  t.Type = 0;
```

Issue 715 already reserved the id space for generated spells: **9,000,000 and
up**, chosen because 3.3.5a's highest real spell id is near 80,000 and custom
modules conventionally sit in 100,000-999,999. This issue should take a
disjoint block inside that range rather than invent its own convention.

### The delivery problem — nothing wanders by right now

This issue assumes a trainer walks up to the player, because phase 5 is built
on travellers rather than towns. That assumption does not currently hold, and
it was checked rather than taken on faith.

**The traveller system is not running on the active profile.** `.profile` says
`vanilla`. E001 symlinks `src/lua-vanilla/` into the worldserver's
`lua_scripts/custom/`, and `src/lua-vanilla/` contains exactly one script —
`auto-equip-starter-kit.lua` — plus a README. The whole beta corpus, travel
included, sits in `src/lua-beta/` where vanilla never looks, and inside that
directory `travel.lua` is filed as `travel.lua.disabled` in any case. No
traveller of any kind spawns for a vanilla player today.

**How the timing works when it does run.** `periodic_events.lua` sets three
spawn clocks as plain globals and registers them per-player on login:

| Constant | Value | What it spawns |
| -------- | ----- | -------------- |
| `DELAY_PERIODIC_SPAWN_CREATURE` | 40 s | an ambush monster |
| `DELAY_PERIODIC_SPAWN_TRAVELLER` | 130 s | one traveller |
| `DELAY_PERIODIC_SPAWN_TREASURE` | 100 s | a treasure chest |

The traveller clock is a flat 130 seconds — unlike the ambush interval, which
random-walks. Each firing re-registers itself and then calls
`Travel.spawnAndTravel`, skipping silently if the player is dead, swimming, or
sitting. The traveller spawns 30 to 45 yards away, walks a course away from
the player, wanders on a 2-4 second step, mirrors the player's sit state, and
despawns after five minutes or when no player is within 200 yards.

**Which traveller appears is a drained bag, not a dice roll.** On login,
`Travel.updatePlayerTravellers` walks ten categories, keeps every entry whose
faction and level bracket fit the player, and builds a list.
`Travel.getRandomTravellerId` then `table.remove`s a random element each time —
so a given trainer appears once and is gone until the list empties and
regenerates. Over a session the player sees the whole eligible cast rather
than the same NPC repeatedly.

**The ten categories, and what is missing from them:**

| Category | typeMask | Category | typeMask |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Ammo | 1 | Others | 32 |
| Innkeepers | 2 | Melee | 64 |
| Trainers | 4 | Ranged | 128 |
| Consumables | 8 | Armor | 256 |
| Poisons | 16 | Food | 512 |

`Travel.getTravelerTypeMask` gives each class its own subset — a mage gets
`2+4+8+32+64+256`, no ammo, no poisons, no food.

Counting the 279 distinct creature ids the travel tables name, against the
world database: **123 class trainers, one weaponsmith, and no profession
trainers at all.** There is no alchemy, blacksmithing, enchanting,
engineering, leatherworking, tailoring, herbalism, mining, skinning, cooking,
first aid or fishing trainer anywhere in the traveller pool, and no category
that would hold one. The "Others" category, which sounds like it might, is
five novelty vendors — an exotic-pet dealer, a toymaker and his clockwork
assistant, a flower vendor, and a cat.

**A weapon-trainer list exists and is dead code.** `wep_trainers` holds twelve
entries — eleven Weapon Masters and Kradu Grimblade, the one Weaponsmith
Trainer — each set to levels 1-20. It is never added to `Travel.travellers`,
so it has no typeMask, so nothing ever reads it. Those twelve NPCs cannot
spawn. Given issue 717 wants universal weapon skills, this list is either the
seed of that feature or something to delete; it should not stay as it is.

The consequence for this issue: **502 has no delivery vehicle on the active
profile**, and even on beta it would reach class trainers only. Whether that
is a blocker or simply the order things get built in is Open Question 5.

## Suggested Implementation Steps

1. **Add the four settings.** Append four entries before
   `MAX_NUM_SERVER_CONFIGS` in `WorldConfig.h`; add four `SetConfigValue`
   calls in `WorldConfig::BuildConfigCache`, all `Reloadable::Yes` so the
   knobs can be turned on a live server while testing balance. This is a
   `patches/B*.sh` source patch — sed-anchored on `MAX_NUM_SERVER_CONFIGS`
   for the enum and on a neighbouring `SetConfigValue` line for the binding,
   with an unpatch that deletes both — following the shape of
   `patches/B005-accuracy-level-cap.sh`.

2. **Lift gate one.** In `Trainer::IsTrainerValidForPlayer`, before the
   existing switch, return true when `Trainer.AnyClass` is on and the
   trainer's `Type` bit is set in `TypeMask`. The two call sites in
   `NPCHandler.cpp` and `PlayerGossip.cpp` then both open up together, so
   the gossip line and the window stay consistent.

3. **Lift gate two.** In `Trainer::SendSpells` and `Trainer::GetSpellState`,
   treat a failing `IsSpellFitByClassAndRace` as passing when the config is
   on and the trainer type qualifies. Keep the level, skill-rank, prerequisite
   and rank-chain checks exactly as they are — the note asks to remove the
   class wall, not the progression.

4. **Price the surcharge.** In `Trainer::SendSpells` (so the window shows the
   real number) and `Trainer::TeachSpell` (so the charge matches the window),
   multiply `MoneyCost` by the surcharge when the spell would have failed the
   class-fit test. The escalator term needs a count of out-of-class spells the
   player already knows; deciding where that count lives — recomputed by
   walking the spellbook, or carried as a character variable — is Open
   Question 3.

5. **Add the config keys to the installed conf.** A `config/patches/C*.sh`
   patch gated to the profiles that want it, following the append-and-marker
   shape of `config/patches/C016-missing-server-config-keys.sh`. Which
   profiles is Open Question 4.

6. **Write the patch doc.** `docs/patches/trainer-any-class.md`, in the
   structure the project's CLAUDE.md lays out, so the C++ change survives an
   upstream re-clone as a description rather than as a diff.

7. **Test the two gates separately.** They fail differently and a combined
   test hides which one broke: one check that a warrior sees the training
   gossip line on a mage trainer (gate one), one that the window is non-empty
   (gate two), one that the price shown equals the price charged, and one
   that a mage's own spells still cost the unmodified amount.

8. **Patch the runic-power gap first.** Extend the switch in
   `SpellInfo::CalcPowerCost` so `POWER_RUNIC_POWER` takes a percentage of
   `GetMaxPower` the way rage and energy already do, instead of logging "Not
   implemented yet!" and charging nothing. Four lines in a `patches/B*.sh`.
   It goes first because without it every generated spell is free for death
   knights, and a generator run before this lands produces data that is
   silently wrong for one class in ten.

9. **Calibrate the percentages against one reference spell per class.** The
   target is one converted cast costing roughly what one native spell of
   similar function costs its own class. That is a comparison, not a formula,
   so it is done by picking the reference spells and reading their costs as
   percentages out of the live database, then writing the table. Rage needs
   its budget measured across the engagement rather than at its start, for
   the reason given above.

10. **Write the generator.** A script — not a hand-maintained SQL file — that
    reads the class-trainer spell list out of the world database, and for each
    spell emits nine siblings, one per other class, into `spell_dbc`: every
    field copied verbatim except `PowerType`, which becomes the target class's
    power, and the cost fields, which become `ManaCost = 0` plus the
    `ManaCostPercentage` from step 9. It also emits the parallel `spell_ranks`
    rows so each class's chain of ranks links to itself rather than to the
    original's. Ids come from the block reserved inside 715's 9,000,000+
    range. The generator is the deliverable; the SQL it produces is a build
    artifact and should be regenerable at any time from the current trainer
    list.

11. **Swap at learn time.** When training completes for an out-of-class spell,
    look up the caller's class sibling, unlearn the stock id, learn the
    sibling. `Trainer::TeachSpell` is where training completes and where the
    swap goes; whether that is a source patch there or an ALE hook on the
    learn event is Open Question 4.

12. **Get the siblings into the client.** Generate the matching `Spell.dbc`
    rows into a client patch archive and add it to whatever players already
    install alongside the AIO addon. This is the same client-side dependency
    issue 715 carries; the two should produce one archive between them, not
    two. Blocked on resolving the disagreement in Open Question 3 about how
    badly the client fails without it.

13. **Price one spell by hand and cast it as two classes.** Frostbolt, given a
    warrior sibling costing a percentage of rage, learned by a warrior and
    cast. This is the smallest thing that proves the whole chain —
    generation, swap, client data, cast — and each of those four can fail
    distinguishably, so check them in that order rather than concluding from
    the button not lighting up. Cast it once at the opening of a fight, when
    the warrior holds no rage, and once mid-fight: the first should refuse and
    the second should land, which is the rhythm working rather than a bug.

