## Open Questions

- **Which profile gets it?** Ritz, 2026-09-25: "It's probably for some
  other profile though so we should leave that as an open question. The
  nice thing is we don't have to choose in order to build it, we can just
  make it as a patch and apply it as we please." So it is built as a
  reversible patch (the worldserver config option the origin note asks
  for, plus its source and SQL patches), listed in no profile until one
  is chosen.

### Answered

- **The resource wall** — 2026-09-03. Not solved at cast time. Each class gets
  a generated copy of the spell with its own power type and converted cost;
  the stock spell is unlearned and the copy learned in its place. Written up
  under *How the cost gets rewritten*.
- **The three unmatched currency names** — 2026-09-03. Names were approximate,
  the count was right, so they are mapped onto the three unnamed engine slots
  in the order given: tiempo to focus, james to happiness, important to runic
  power. Table under *The seven slots*.
- **Scope** — 2026-09-03. **Class trainers only.** `TypeMask` stays at 1.
  Mount trainers keep their race requirement, pet trainers keep their class
  requirement, profession trainers keep their prerequisite spell.
- **How rage is priced** — 2026-09-03. Rage takes the runic-power paradigm,
  not the energy one. Rage and runic power are both accrual currencies, get
  one formula between them, and are budgeted across the engagement rather than
  off the bar. Table under *Rage is priced as runic power*.
- **How a spell is assigned to a spec** — 2026-09-03. By the colour of the
  spell's own icon against the three spec-icon anchors, nearest wins: hue
  distance, gated on the icon's saturation clearing about 30% so near-grey
  icons are not assigned at random. Measured on twenty spells and it
  reproduces the brief's own examples — green nature spells land on unholy,
  fire lands on blood, gold Holy Light lands on blood. Generalises to every
  class, since `TalentTab.dbc` gives every spec an icon. Tables under *Runes
  and runic power*.
- **How a profile gets the travel system** — 2026-09-04. By symlink from the
  beta corpus, with beta as upstream: *"we can do symlink, and let the upstream
  handle patches to that branch."* Profile-specific differences — which spawn
  clocks register, which traveller categories are eligible — are conditionals
  inside the shared files rather than forks of them. The same decision is
  recorded in 153d, which needed it too.
- **Why the weapon-trainer list is dead** — confirmed 2026-09-04. It is not an
  oversight; weapon training was removed on purpose. Issue 148h, *Weapon
  Skills — Full Proficiency, Starter Weapon Sharpened*, grants every character
  proficiency in every weapon its class can learn, in its own words *"so the
  player never visits a weapon master"*. It ships in
  `src/lua-vanilla/auto-equip-starter-kit.lua` on `PLAYER_EVENT_ON_FIRST_LOGIN`,
  driven by a per-class `CLASS_WEAPON_SKILLS` table derived from live character
  data and a `WEAPON_SKILL_PROFICIENCY` map of skill line to proficiency spell.
  So the twelve unregistered Weapon Masters in `travel.lua` are the residue of
  a shipped decision. **Caveat: the hook is vanilla-only** — it lives in
  `src/lua-vanilla/`, so on beta nothing grants proficiencies and weapon
  masters would still have work to do. Whether beta gets the same treatment is
  a live question rather than a settled one.
- **What "an equivalent amount" means** — 2026-09-03. A percentage of what
  that unit spends across the engagement, not an exchange rate between pools,
  calibrated so one converted cast costs about what one native spell of
  similar function costs. The engine already carries this as
  `ManaCostPercentage`, so the generator writes a percentage and the engine
  converts per caster. Written up under *"An equivalent amount"*.

### Deferred by decision

Set aside deliberately on 2026-09-04 — *"ask me those questions when we get to
them."* Not open in the sense of blocking, and not answered. They are asked
again when somebody is actually writing the cost table, which is the first
moment either answer changes anything.

- **What "the top two" ranks, now that the colour rule is understood.** The
  colours turned out to be anchors for matching spells, not a ranking of the
  specs — so the remaining half of the rule, that powerful high-resource
  spells take "one of the top two" runes, still needs an ordering from
  somewhere, and it is no longer obvious it comes from colour at all. Likely
  candidates: an ordering by how much resource the spell demands (the two
  most expensive rune types for the most expensive spells), or the talent
  tab `OrderIndex` the game already carries (blood 0, frost 1, unholy 2).

- **The cooldown split names runic power on both branches.** As given: short
  cooldowns of four or six seconds cost runic power, and no-cooldown or
  long-cooldown spells cost runic power. One branch was meant to be runes.
  Which one? The runes are themselves cooldown-shaped — each recharges on a
  timer rather than draining from a pool — which reads as an argument for
  short-cooldown spells costing runes and no-cooldown spells costing
  accumulated runic power, but that is a guess and not a decision.


### Still open

1. **The letter notation.** Five coefficients take their currency's first
   letter (T tiempo, J james, I important, R rage, R runes); mana and energy
   take each other's (N mana, M energy), and R does double duty for rage and
   runes. Placeholders, or a notation with rules? If it is a notation it needs
   writing down before the conversion table is built, because the conversion
   table is where those coefficients become numbers.

2. **The reference spell for each class.** The conversion rule is settled —
   percentage of the engagement, calibrated so one converted cast costs about
   what one native spell of similar function costs. What is not settled is
   *which* spell plays "similar function" for each of the ten classes. That
   choice is the calibration, and picking it wrong tilts a whole class. It
   needs a person who knows how each class actually plays, which makes it a
   `tissues/` question rather than an `issues/` one.

3. **Whether the missing delivery vehicle blocks this.** The traveller system
   is not loaded on the vanilla profile at all, travel.lua is filed as
   `.disabled` even in the beta corpus, and no profession trainer exists in
   the traveller pool on any profile. So even a finished 502 reaches nobody
   today. Is the order of work: turn travellers back on first, or build 502
   against a system that is switched off and turn both on together?

4. **How badly the client fails on an unknown spell id.** This file says a
   spell absent from the client's `Spell.dbc` cannot appear in the spellbook
   at all, because the spellbook is drawn from client data and the server
   sends only ids. Issue 715 says tooltips "will be wrong" — which implies the
   spell still appears. Both cannot be true, neither has been checked in-game,
   and the difference decides whether the client patch archive is polish or a
   hard dependency. Check before building anything downstream of it.

5. **Where the swap lives.** `Trainer::TeachSpell` in the core is the exact
   moment training completes, which argues for a source patch. But the project
   prefers Lua where Lua reaches, and ALE has a learn hook. A core patch is
   one more thing to re-derive on every upstream re-clone; a Lua hook risks
   the player briefly holding the wrong spell between the two events. Which
   cost is preferable?

6. **What happens to a sibling when its original is patched.** Upstream
   rebalances a spell, or one of this project's own SQL files does. The nine
   copies are now stale and nothing notices. Does the generator re-run on
   every build (regenerating from current data, which is the project's stated
   preference for generated things), or does it run once and drift?

7. **Whether siblings are unlearned when the config is turned off.** An
   operator flips `Trainer.AnyClass` back to 0. Characters are still holding
   generated spells that no trainer will now sell. Left alone, or swept?

8. **Where the escalator's count lives.** `CostPerKnown` needs to know how many
   out-of-class spells a character already owns. Recomputing it by walking the
   spellbook on every trainer window is correct but touches every spell every
   time; caching it as a character variable is cheap but can drift when spells
   are learned outside a trainer (tomes, quests, GM commands). Which failure
   is preferable?

9. **Which profiles turn it on.** Vanilla is the active profile and the one
   whose design most wants this. Should release and alpha get the keys at
   default-off, or not get them at all?

10. **How this and 717 agree on the price of universality.** 717 charges for
   universal weapons with 3x slower talent training; this charges for
   universal spells with gold at the trainer and with a converted resource
   cost at every cast. If both ship, a character pays three times in three
   currencies for one idea. Intended, or should they collapse into one price?

11. **Whether 502 and 715 share one generator.** Both generate spell siblings
    into the 9,000,000+ range, both need a client patch archive, both swap by
    unlearn-and-relearn. 715 multiplies by level, this multiplies by class. If
    they stay separate they will each build the same three machines; if they
    merge, the generated set is level x class and the count question in *How
    many spells this makes* needs answering first.

12. **What the playerbots do with it.** Bots visit trainers and train what they
    can afford. With the class gate off, a bot warrior spends its gold on mage
    spells — and under the sibling design those spells now *work*, so bot
    combat behaviour changes rather than merely wasting money. Does the bot
    training logic need to keep the class check even when players do not?

13. **Whether the client's spellbook tabs cooperate.** The 3.3.5 client sorts
    the spellbook by skill line. A warrior holding a warrior-flavoured
    Frostbolt has no arcane tab to put it in. Since the siblings are generated,
    their skill line is a field we choose — which may make this a non-issue, or
    may mean choosing between "appears under a mage tab that shouldn't exist"
    and "appears under General with everything else". Needs looking at in-game
    alongside Question 3.
